1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:09,436
In the next thirty minutes, you will watch the sun swallow the Earth, the last star flicker out, and the final black hole in existence evaporate into nothing.

2
00:00:10,367 --> 00:00:17,760
And at the very end, in the dark after everything, you will see why scientists think this story might begin again.

3
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,111
This is mindwired.

4
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:23,435
Timelapse of the end of the universe.

5
00:00:23,435 --> 00:00:25,317
A journey to the last moment.

6
00:00:26,267 --> 00:00:27,687
Here is how this works.

7
00:00:27,687 --> 00:00:31,021
We are going to run the clock forward, and never stop.

8
00:00:31,967 --> 00:00:33,213
Watch the counter.

9
00:00:33,213 --> 00:00:36,329
Every few seconds, the speed of time doubles.

10
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:40,711
At first, centuries will crawl past.

11
00:00:40,711 --> 00:00:42,145
Then millennia.

12
00:00:42,145 --> 00:00:44,346
Then the ages of stars.

13
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:49,943
By the end, a trillion years will pass in the space of a single breath.

14
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:56,013
Everything you are about to see is real physics, projected forward by working cosmologists.

15
00:00:56,933 --> 00:01:01,604
Some of it was only discovered in the last two years, and it changes the ending.

16
00:01:01,604 --> 00:01:02,654
We will get there.

17
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:07,333
Where the science is still a hypothesis, we will say so.

18
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:09,399
The rest is settled arithmetic.

19
00:01:10,333 --> 00:01:13,918
Take one last look at the universe as it is now.

20
00:01:13,918 --> 00:01:16,681
Bright, warm, and crowded with stars.

21
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,638
You will not see it like this again.

22
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:22,213
Ten thousand years from now.

23
00:01:23,133 --> 00:01:30,839
Ten thousand years is longer than every written word, every city, every recorded human civilization combined.

24
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:33,114
And yet, look up.

25
00:01:33,114 --> 00:01:36,286
The night sky has barely changed at all.

26
00:01:37,233 --> 00:01:42,301
The constellations have drifted slightly out of shape, like a photograph left in the sun.

27
00:01:43,233 --> 00:01:45,113
Orion's shoulders have shifted.

28
00:01:45,113 --> 00:01:48,144
The Big Dipper's handle has bent into a new curve.

29
00:01:49,067 --> 00:01:54,945
Down on Earth, Niagara Falls has eroded itself out of existence, cutting all the way back to its lake.

30
00:01:55,867 --> 00:02:00,700
Every satellite we ever launched into low orbit has long since fallen and burned.

31
00:02:01,633 --> 00:02:06,309
The pyramids, if anything remains of them, are now twice as old as they were.

32
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:11,909
Voyager 1, still coasting outward, has traveled less than half a light year from home.

33
00:02:12,833 --> 00:02:18,084
On this timescale, humanity's entire story so far is the first frame of the film.

34
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:21,419
The North Star has changed, too.

35
00:02:21,419 --> 00:02:26,190
Earth's axis wobbles like a spinning top, tracing a slow circle.

36
00:02:27,133 --> 00:02:32,384
Polaris has handed the job to another star, the way it inherited it from one before.

37
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:35,571
The stars have not noticed us yet.

38
00:02:35,571 --> 00:02:38,401
Ten thousand years means nothing to a star.

39
00:02:39,333 --> 00:02:44,322
A red dwarf like Proxima Centauri will burn for trillions more years without blinking.

40
00:02:45,267 --> 00:02:46,667
Small things drift.

41
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:47,846
Big things wait.

42
00:02:47,846 --> 00:02:51,458
That is the rhythm of the universe at this scale.

43
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:56,998
But what happens when we stop counting in thousands, and start counting in millions?

44
00:02:57,933 --> 00:02:59,344
One million years from now.

45
00:03:00,267 --> 00:03:01,902
Betelgeuse is long gone.

46
00:03:01,902 --> 00:03:06,876
The red supergiant in Orion detonated hundreds of thousands of years ago.

47
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:12,476
For weeks, its supernova burned in Earth's sky brighter than the full moon.

48
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:19,356
It was visible in broad daylight, a second sun hanging on the shoulder of a broken constellation.

49
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:26,099
Now only a spinning corpse remains, a neutron star flickering where a giant once stood.

50
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:32,022
The constellations you learned as a child no longer exist in any recognizable form.

51
00:03:32,967 --> 00:03:34,910
Every star has wandered.

52
00:03:34,910 --> 00:03:37,016
The sky is a stranger now.

53
00:03:37,933 --> 00:03:42,551
On Earth, Meteor Crater in Arizona has eroded away completely.

54
00:03:42,551 --> 00:03:44,934
The planet erases its own scars.

55
00:03:45,867 --> 00:03:52,215
No structure built by human hands survives one million years of rain, ice, and plate tectonics.

56
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:53,941
Except two.

57
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:59,742
The Voyager probes still drift between the stars, dead and perfectly preserved.

58
00:04:00,667 --> 00:04:07,433
Their golden records, built to last a billion years, may now be the oldest human-made objects in existence.

59
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:12,808
Two whispers in an ocean of night, outlasting everything ever built on Earth.

60
00:04:13,733 --> 00:04:17,424
The moon has drifted about thirty meters farther away.

61
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:20,499
It leaves us slowly, four centimeters a year.

62
00:04:21,433 --> 00:04:25,952
If our descendants are still out there, they live under a sky we would not recognize.

63
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:29,116
And this is still the very beginning.

64
00:04:29,116 --> 00:04:31,811
What does one hundred times longer look like?

65
00:04:32,733 --> 00:04:34,927
One hundred million years from now.

66
00:04:35,867 --> 00:04:37,748
Saturn is losing its crown.

67
00:04:38,667 --> 00:04:40,458
The rings are not permanent.

68
00:04:40,458 --> 00:04:45,511
They are younger than the dinosaurs, and they are raining down onto the planet.

69
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:54,244
Ring particles, tugged by gravity and charged by sunlight, spiral inward as a steady icy rain.

70
00:04:55,167 --> 00:05:01,123
Within a few hundred million years, the most beautiful structure in the solar system simply dissolves.

71
00:05:02,067 --> 00:05:03,011
We were lucky.

72
00:05:03,011 --> 00:05:07,056
We existed in the brief window when Saturn had rings at all.

73
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,474
Earth, meanwhile, has been playing cosmic roulette.

74
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:19,244
Over one hundred million years, there is roughly a coin-flip chance of another impact like the one that killed the dinosaurs.

75
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:21,911
The continents have wandered.

76
00:05:21,911 --> 00:05:27,324
The Atlantic is wider, the Pacific narrower, and new mountains rise where seas used to be.

77
00:05:28,267 --> 00:05:33,674
Whatever maps of Earth ever existed, they now describe a planet that no longer exists.

78
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:39,354
Out in the galaxy, our solar system has drifted through entire spiral arms.

79
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:44,440
Dinosaurs to now, that was one hundred million years.

80
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:48,111
Run that whole story again, and we arrive here.

81
00:05:49,033 --> 00:05:52,524
The sun burns on, but it is quietly getting hotter.

82
00:05:52,524 --> 00:05:54,440
And that changes everything.

83
00:05:55,367 --> 00:05:58,684
Because the next stop is where Earth's story starts to end.

84
00:05:59,633 --> 00:06:01,357
One billion years from now.

85
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:06,548
The sun is now about ten percent brighter than it was in your lifetime.

86
00:06:06,548 --> 00:06:07,446
That is enough.

87
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:09,856
Enough to break the Earth.

88
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,736
The oceans are evaporating.

89
00:06:12,736 --> 00:06:18,114
Water vapor floods the upper atmosphere, trapping heat in a runaway spiral.

90
00:06:19,033 --> 00:06:25,381
Sunlight splits the vapor apart, and the hydrogen leaks away into space, atom by atom.

91
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:30,584
The seas that birthed every living thing are boiling off into the dark.

92
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:38,299
Earth is becoming a desert world, its ocean basins cracked open like dried mud under a furious sky.

93
00:06:39,233 --> 00:06:42,868
By 1.3 billion years, complex life is finished.

94
00:06:42,868 --> 00:06:47,122
Only single-celled microbes remain, hiding underground.

95
00:06:48,067 --> 00:06:51,162
Life on Earth ends the way it began.

96
00:06:51,162 --> 00:06:54,258
Alone, microscopic, and in the dark.

97
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,156
Here is the cruel accounting.

98
00:06:57,156 --> 00:07:01,809
Earth was habitable for roughly five and a half billion years, total.

99
00:07:02,733 --> 00:07:05,991
Life used almost all of it just getting to brains.

100
00:07:05,991 --> 00:07:08,532
We arrived minutes before closing time.

101
00:07:09,467 --> 00:07:12,393
Meanwhile, the outer solar system is warming.

102
00:07:13,333 --> 00:07:18,087
The icy moons of Jupiter and Saturn, frozen for eons, begin to soften.

103
00:07:19,033 --> 00:07:23,787
For a brief window, moons like Europa may hold liquid water on their surfaces.

104
00:07:24,733 --> 00:07:30,219
The habitable zone is migrating outward, like a campfire's warmth reaching the back rows.

105
00:07:31,167 --> 00:07:35,921
Life's last refuge in this solar system may not be a planet at all, but a moon.

106
00:07:36,867 --> 00:07:40,207
But refuges only last as long as the campfire.

107
00:07:40,207 --> 00:07:43,476
And the campfire is about to explode in size.

108
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:46,438
Four billion years from now.

109
00:07:47,367 --> 00:07:51,729
For decades, every documentary told you the same story about this moment.

110
00:07:52,667 --> 00:07:58,587
That the Andromeda galaxy, a trillion stars strong, would collide with the Milky Way.

111
00:07:58,587 --> 00:07:59,354
Guaranteed.

112
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:03,077
The textbooks said it was certain.

113
00:08:03,077 --> 00:08:06,099
Then, in 2025, we re-ran the numbers.

114
00:08:07,033 --> 00:08:15,627
A study in Nature Astronomy, using better data on our neighboring galaxies, found the odds are only about fifty-fifty within ten billion years.

115
00:08:16,567 --> 00:08:19,927
The most famous collision in astronomy is a coin flip.

116
00:08:19,927 --> 00:08:23,724
The universe kept that secret through a century of textbooks.

117
00:08:24,667 --> 00:08:26,107
So flip the coin.

118
00:08:26,107 --> 00:08:28,141
Heads, and this happens.

119
00:08:29,067 --> 00:08:34,318
Two spiral galaxies, hundreds of billions of suns each, falling through one another.

120
00:08:35,267 --> 00:08:37,428
Almost no stars actually hit.

121
00:08:37,428 --> 00:08:38,843
Space is too empty.

122
00:08:38,843 --> 00:08:42,346
The stars pass like ships in a harbor at night.

123
00:08:43,267 --> 00:08:45,290
But gravity rewrites everything.

124
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:50,033
Spiral arms are torn loose and flung across a hundred thousand light years.

125
00:08:50,967 --> 00:08:53,735
Gas clouds slam together and ignite.

126
00:08:53,735 --> 00:08:57,733
A firestorm of newborn stars lights up the wreckage.

127
00:08:58,667 --> 00:09:04,780
The two galaxies swing apart, fall back, and merge into one enormous elliptical blur.

128
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:08,835
Astronomers named the child Milkdromeda.

129
00:09:08,835 --> 00:09:11,264
One galaxy, made of two ghosts.

130
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:18,809
Tails, and the giants waltz past each other, still bound, saving the merger for a later eon.

131
00:09:19,733 --> 00:09:22,375
Either way, they likely end up as one.

132
00:09:22,375 --> 00:09:24,252
The coin only decides when.

133
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:29,798
But while the galaxies dance, something much closer to home is running out of time.

134
00:09:30,733 --> 00:09:32,300
Eight billion years from now.

135
00:09:33,233 --> 00:09:36,830
The sun's core has exhausted the last of its hydrogen.

136
00:09:36,830 --> 00:09:38,562
And the sun begins to die.

137
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:41,812
It does not go quietly.

138
00:09:41,812 --> 00:09:42,817
It swells.

139
00:09:43,767 --> 00:09:51,656
At its peak, about 7.9 billion years from now, the sun is 256 times wider than the star you were born under.

140
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:54,146
Mercury is swallowed.

141
00:09:54,146 --> 00:09:55,546
Venus is swallowed.

142
00:09:55,546 --> 00:09:59,522
The sun's boiling surface climbs toward Earth's orbit.

143
00:10:00,467 --> 00:10:05,718
And around 7.6 billion years from now, most models agree, it reaches us.

144
00:10:06,667 --> 00:10:08,688
Earth's thin escape routes fail.

145
00:10:08,688 --> 00:10:11,656
Tidal drag pulls the planet down into the fire.

146
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:16,861
The world where everything you have ever loved happened, ends here.

147
00:10:16,861 --> 00:10:19,914
Dissolved into the outer layers of its own star.

148
00:10:20,833 --> 00:10:25,901
The dying sun exhales those layers into space, a glowing shroud of gas.

149
00:10:26,833 --> 00:10:33,520
For a few thousand years, our star becomes a planetary nebula, one of the most beautiful objects in the galaxy.

150
00:10:34,467 --> 00:10:38,329
Then the light fades, and only the core remains.

151
00:10:38,329 --> 00:10:39,456
A white dwarf.

152
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:46,434
An Earth-sized ember holding 54 percent of the sun's mass, a million times denser than water.

153
00:10:47,367 --> 00:10:52,696
Somewhere in its ash are the atoms that used to be Mercury, Venus, and Earth.

154
00:10:53,633 --> 00:10:58,466
Everything the inner solar system ever was, filed away in a stellar ash heap.

155
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:01,390
The outer planets remain.

156
00:11:01,390 --> 00:11:06,166
Jupiter, Saturn, Neptune, loyal and dark, orbiting a corpse.

157
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:11,698
A visitor arriving now would never guess this system once hosted a civilization.

158
00:11:12,633 --> 00:11:14,567
Unless they found the probes.

159
00:11:14,567 --> 00:11:17,701
Two golden records, still waiting to be played.

160
00:11:18,633 --> 00:11:20,539
The sun does not explode.

161
00:11:20,539 --> 00:11:23,130
It is not massive enough for that.

162
00:11:23,130 --> 00:11:26,026
It simply retires, and starts to cool.

163
00:11:26,967 --> 00:11:31,016
It will spend longer as this fading ember than it ever spent as a star.

164
00:11:31,933 --> 00:11:36,609
Our main character is dead, and the story is not even one percent finished.

165
00:11:37,533 --> 00:11:41,739
So what happens to a universe that keeps expanding after its stars start dying?

166
00:11:42,667 --> 00:11:44,626
One hundred billion years from now.

167
00:11:45,567 --> 00:11:49,694
The universe is now seven times older than it was when humans existed.

168
00:11:50,633 --> 00:11:53,940
And something terrible has happened to the sky.

169
00:11:53,940 --> 00:11:54,995
It is emptying.

170
00:11:55,933 --> 00:12:01,732
Dark energy has been stretching space faster and faster, and distant galaxies are losing the race.

171
00:12:02,667 --> 00:12:08,309
One by one, they cross the cosmic horizon, the point beyond which their light can never reach us again.

172
00:12:09,233 --> 00:12:11,254
They are not destroyed.

173
00:12:11,254 --> 00:12:13,363
They are simply cut off.

174
00:12:13,363 --> 00:12:14,066
Forever.

175
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:20,878
Watch a distant galaxy approach the horizon and its light stretches, reddens, and freezes.

176
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:26,946
Its final image hangs in the sky like a photograph, fading slowly to black.

177
00:12:27,867 --> 00:12:31,395
Trillions of galaxies exit the universe this way.

178
00:12:31,395 --> 00:12:32,043
Politely.

179
00:12:32,043 --> 00:12:33,196
Without a sound.

180
00:12:34,133 --> 00:12:39,384
Every galaxy beyond our local group has now vanished past the edge of the observable universe.

181
00:12:40,333 --> 00:12:44,199
Milkdromeda has swallowed its remaining neighbors and floats alone in the dark.

182
00:12:45,133 --> 00:12:51,167
An astronomer born in this era, with perfect telescopes, would see one galaxy and then nothing.

183
00:12:52,100 --> 00:12:54,436
They could never discover the Big Bang.

184
00:12:54,436 --> 00:12:57,011
The evidence has physically left their sky.

185
00:12:57,933 --> 00:13:02,687
They would be geniuses, and they would be wrong, because the truth abandoned them.

186
00:13:03,633 --> 00:13:05,861
Think about what that means for us.

187
00:13:05,861 --> 00:13:08,152
What evidence has already left ours?

188
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:12,209
Knowledge in this universe has an expiration date.

189
00:13:12,209 --> 00:13:14,821
We are reading the library while it burns.

190
00:13:15,767 --> 00:13:19,363
And the stars themselves are now on their final generation.

191
00:13:19,363 --> 00:13:21,801
Who lights the lamps after the last one?

192
00:13:22,733 --> 00:13:24,457
One trillion years from now.

193
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:27,202
The galaxy is running out of fuel.

194
00:13:28,133 --> 00:13:30,426
Here is a secret about our own time.

195
00:13:30,426 --> 00:13:34,246
The universe's star-birth rate peaked ten billion years ago.

196
00:13:35,167 --> 00:13:37,715
You were not born at the dawn of the universe.

197
00:13:37,715 --> 00:13:41,593
You were born in its epilogue, while the credits were already rolling.

198
00:13:42,533 --> 00:13:44,918
Now even the epilogue is ending.

199
00:13:44,918 --> 00:13:49,612
Star formation needs cold gas, and the cold gas is nearly gone.

200
00:13:50,533 --> 00:13:55,522
Somewhere in Milkdromeda, one of the last star-forming clouds collapses and ignites.

201
00:13:56,467 --> 00:14:02,501
A handful of final newborn stars, small and red, lighting up in a galaxy of retirees.

202
00:14:03,433 --> 00:14:09,311
These late children are red dwarfs, tiny misers that sip their fuel for trillions of years.

203
00:14:10,233 --> 00:14:13,334
The universe's strategy has changed.

204
00:14:13,334 --> 00:14:14,799
No more bonfires.

205
00:14:14,799 --> 00:14:17,469
Only candles, rationed to last.

206
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:22,053
Strange collisions still light the darkness now and then.

207
00:14:22,053 --> 00:14:25,322
Two dead white dwarfs spiral together and reignite.

208
00:14:26,267 --> 00:14:31,596
For a moment, a new star burns where no star should exist, assembled from corpses.

209
00:14:32,533 --> 00:14:37,131
These resurrection stars will be the only new lights for trillions of years to come.

210
00:14:38,067 --> 00:14:44,101
The age of abundant starlight, the Stelliferous Era, is entering its long final act.

211
00:14:45,033 --> 00:14:48,168
Every star that will ever be born now exists.

212
00:14:48,168 --> 00:14:50,675
From here, the count only goes down.

213
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:56,082
Down, and down, until one night there is exactly one star left.

214
00:14:56,082 --> 00:14:58,287
What does that night look like?

215
00:14:59,233 --> 00:15:01,427
One hundred trillion years from now.

216
00:15:02,367 --> 00:15:04,640
This is the night the last star dies.

217
00:15:05,567 --> 00:15:10,974
Somewhere in the darkness, a final red dwarf has been burning for trillions of years.

218
00:15:11,900 --> 00:15:13,989
Red dwarfs do not explode.

219
00:15:13,989 --> 00:15:17,122
They do not even swell into red giants.

220
00:15:17,122 --> 00:15:20,416
They are too small, too careful for that.

221
00:15:21,333 --> 00:15:28,882
This one has stirred every last atom of its hydrogen into the fire, grown hotter, bluer, and simply run dry.

222
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:33,274
Its light flickers, dims, and goes out.

223
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:36,544
And with it ends the era of stars.

224
00:15:36,544 --> 00:15:40,887
Ten thousand times longer than all of cosmic history before it.

225
00:15:41,833 --> 00:15:43,880
Nothing shines anymore.

226
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:44,680
Anywhere.

227
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,754
The universe now contains only remnants.

228
00:15:48,754 --> 00:15:54,351
White dwarfs, neutron stars, black holes, and frozen wandering planets.

229
00:15:55,300 --> 00:16:01,021
Cosmologists call what comes next the Degenerate Era, named for the exhausted matter that remains.

230
00:16:01,967 --> 00:16:06,643
If you were standing on one of the surviving planets now, this is what you would see.

231
00:16:07,567 --> 00:16:08,221
Nothing.

232
00:16:08,221 --> 00:16:12,635
A sky of perfect black, no stars, no glow, no horizon.

233
00:16:12,635 --> 00:16:16,396
Your own hand invisible in front of your face.

234
00:16:17,333 --> 00:16:24,255
Under your feet, a world frozen within a whisper of absolute zero, holding the fossils of an atmosphere.

235
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:27,056
The neutron stars persist.

236
00:16:27,056 --> 00:16:31,626
City-sized spheres of pure nuclear matter, spinning in the dark.

237
00:16:32,567 --> 00:16:37,243
Some still pulse their lighthouse beams across a universe with no ships left to warn.

238
00:16:38,167 --> 00:16:43,313
Even the magnetars, the most violent magnets nature ever built, have gone quiet.

239
00:16:44,233 --> 00:16:50,424
If life still exists, it huddles around white dwarfs, harvesting the last warmth in the cosmos.

240
00:16:51,367 --> 00:16:53,203
But even embers cool.

241
00:16:53,203 --> 00:16:55,651
And the darkness is patient.

242
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:01,511
What does gravity do with a universe full of corpses and infinite time to play with?

243
00:17:02,433 --> 00:17:05,322
Ten to the power twenty years from now.

244
00:17:05,322 --> 00:17:07,840
One hundred billion billion years.

245
00:17:08,767 --> 00:17:11,118
The galaxies themselves are dissolving.

246
00:17:12,067 --> 00:17:18,101
Over these absurd timescales, close encounters between dead stars slowly fling them out of the galaxy.

247
00:17:19,033 --> 00:17:24,597
First the planets are stripped from their dead suns, detached by passing stars and set adrift.

248
00:17:25,533 --> 00:17:27,106
Then the stars themselves.

249
00:17:27,106 --> 00:17:32,612
Somewhere between ninety and ninety-nine percent of all remnants are ejected into the void.

250
00:17:33,533 --> 00:17:37,739
Star by star, Milkdromeda is evaporating into intergalactic space.

251
00:17:38,667 --> 00:17:43,265
The rest spiral inward, and fall into the supermassive black hole at the center.

252
00:17:44,200 --> 00:17:48,641
The black hole grows fat on the wreckage of a hundred billion dead suns.

253
00:17:49,567 --> 00:17:55,680
Rogue black holes cruise the darkness, invisible, swallowing anything unlucky enough to cross them.

254
00:17:56,600 --> 00:17:59,198
The white dwarfs have finished cooling.

255
00:17:59,198 --> 00:18:01,929
They no longer emit visible light at all.

256
00:18:02,867 --> 00:18:04,658
They are black dwarfs now.

257
00:18:04,658 --> 00:18:10,103
Objects so far in our future that not one exists yet, anywhere in the universe.

258
00:18:11,033 --> 00:18:15,082
Each one has cooled to about five degrees above absolute zero.

259
00:18:15,082 --> 00:18:18,347
Colder than your freezer, with the mass of a star.

260
00:18:19,267 --> 00:18:21,475
Put yourself in orbit around one.

261
00:18:21,475 --> 00:18:23,014
You would never see it.

262
00:18:23,014 --> 00:18:29,638
You would only feel it, a blind weight in the dark, swinging you around an invisible point forever.

263
00:18:30,567 --> 00:18:35,095
If Earth's cinder escaped the sun, it drifts out here somewhere.

264
00:18:35,095 --> 00:18:39,553
A silent stone circling a silent ember, going nowhere, forever.

265
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:46,299
The universe has become a museum with the lights off, and gravity is quietly stealing the exhibits.

266
00:18:47,233 --> 00:18:50,071
But the exhibits themselves may not be safe.

267
00:18:50,071 --> 00:18:53,424
Because matter itself might have an expiration date.

268
00:18:54,367 --> 00:18:57,293
Ten to the power thirty-four years and beyond.

269
00:18:58,233 --> 00:19:02,210
Now we reach the deepest question in this entire journey.

270
00:19:02,210 --> 00:19:03,954
Does matter last forever?

271
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:07,486
Many physicists suspect it does not.

272
00:19:07,486 --> 00:19:12,371
They suspect the proton, the heart of every atom, eventually decays.

273
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:14,015
Be clear.

274
00:19:14,015 --> 00:19:15,684
This is a hypothesis.

275
00:19:15,684 --> 00:19:20,928
Proton decay has never been observed, despite decades of watching.

276
00:19:21,867 --> 00:19:30,069
In Japan, fifty thousand tons of ultra-pure water sit in darkness, ringed by thousands of golden light sensors.

277
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:36,642
Scientists have watched that tank for decades, waiting for a single proton to flash goodbye.

278
00:19:37,567 --> 00:19:38,696
Not one ever has.

279
00:19:38,696 --> 00:19:46,736
We now know protons live at least ten to the power thirty-four years, and an even bigger detector is listening right now.

280
00:19:47,667 --> 00:19:54,093
But most grand unified theories of physics insist that eventually, every proton must fall apart.

281
00:19:55,033 --> 00:19:59,709
If they are right, then out here in deep time, the end of matter begins.

282
00:20:00,633 --> 00:20:07,947
Inside every black dwarf, every frozen planet, every drifting rock, protons quietly wink out of existence.

283
00:20:08,867 --> 00:20:11,987
Each decay releases a tiny flash of energy.

284
00:20:11,987 --> 00:20:15,398
The dead stars gain a final, impossible warmth.

285
00:20:16,333 --> 00:20:21,001
Proton decay keeps each black dwarf a few degrees above absolute zero.

286
00:20:21,001 --> 00:20:24,535
That trickle is the last heat source in the universe.

287
00:20:25,467 --> 00:20:30,763
It is less power than a household light bulb, spread across an entire dead star.

288
00:20:30,763 --> 00:20:32,154
And it is everything.

289
00:20:33,100 --> 00:20:40,257
Slowly, atom by atom, every solid object in existence evaporates into positrons and radiation.

290
00:20:41,200 --> 00:20:42,887
The planets dissolve.

291
00:20:42,887 --> 00:20:44,976
The black dwarfs dissolve.

292
00:20:44,976 --> 00:20:49,716
Every monument, every fossil, every atom that was ever you.

293
00:20:50,633 --> 00:20:56,432
By ten to the power forty years, if the theories are right, matter as we know it is over.

294
00:20:57,367 --> 00:20:59,115
And a strange footnote.

295
00:20:59,115 --> 00:21:04,133
If protons never decay, physics has an even slower ending on file.

296
00:21:05,067 --> 00:21:11,336
Over ten to the power fifteen hundred years, quantum tunneling would slowly transmute all matter into iron.

297
00:21:12,267 --> 00:21:17,021
Dead stars would become iron stars, the most stable objects arithmetic allows.

298
00:21:17,967 --> 00:21:21,303
Either way, the destination is the same.

299
00:21:21,303 --> 00:21:24,889
Matter simplifies, settles, and surrenders.

300
00:21:25,833 --> 00:21:27,920
Nothing made of atoms remains.

301
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:30,979
So what is left, when matter itself is gone?

302
00:21:31,900 --> 00:21:34,329
Ten to the power forty years from now.

303
00:21:35,267 --> 00:21:36,253
The black holes.

304
00:21:36,253 --> 00:21:40,753
Only the things that were never made of atoms survive the death of atoms.

305
00:21:41,700 --> 00:21:43,518
Welcome to the Black Hole Era.

306
00:21:43,518 --> 00:21:46,062
The universe now belongs entirely to them.

307
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:53,922
Every galaxy that ever existed is now, at most, a single black hole and a thin fog of particles.

308
00:21:54,867 --> 00:21:57,183
The monsters have inherited everything.

309
00:21:57,183 --> 00:21:59,856
And the largest of them are beyond imagining.

310
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:06,521
Remember TON 618, the sixty-six billion solar mass giant we could see from Earth?

311
00:22:07,467 --> 00:22:13,501
Holes like that now drift through the darkness, horizons wider than entire ancient solar systems.

312
00:22:14,433 --> 00:22:16,231
Nothing orbits them anymore.

313
00:22:16,231 --> 00:22:18,221
There is nothing left to orbit.

314
00:22:19,167 --> 00:22:20,431
They do not feed.

315
00:22:20,431 --> 00:22:21,696
They do not grow.

316
00:22:21,696 --> 00:22:26,011
They simply exist, holding everything they ever swallowed.

317
00:22:26,933 --> 00:22:30,590
An event horizon is the universe's most patient archive.

318
00:22:30,590 --> 00:22:33,202
Whatever fell in is still accounted for.

319
00:22:34,133 --> 00:22:41,134
Somewhere inside them, in whatever form information takes past a horizon, is the ledger of everything that ever fell.

320
00:22:42,067 --> 00:22:44,026
Stars, planets, oceans.

321
00:22:44,026 --> 00:22:47,945
Possibly the atoms of everyone who ever lived.

322
00:22:48,867 --> 00:22:51,871
The black holes are the universe's last libraries.

323
00:22:52,800 --> 00:22:57,633
For a span of time that dwarfs everything that came before, nothing happens at all.

324
00:22:58,567 --> 00:22:59,780
No collisions.

325
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:00,561
No light.

326
00:23:00,561 --> 00:23:01,428
No change.

327
00:23:01,428 --> 00:23:06,456
Just black spheres in black space, outnumbered by nothing.

328
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:10,126
If you think this is the end, you are wrong.

329
00:23:10,126 --> 00:23:12,233
Because black holes have a secret.

330
00:23:13,167 --> 00:23:15,098
Stephen Hawking found it.

331
00:23:15,098 --> 00:23:16,720
They are not eternal.

332
00:23:17,667 --> 00:23:18,660
They leak.

333
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:23,734
And the fire that burns the last libraries has already been lit.

334
00:23:23,734 --> 00:23:25,478
How long can they hold out?

335
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:28,986
Ten to the power sixty-seven years from now.

336
00:23:29,933 --> 00:23:33,982
Quantum physics does not allow even a black hole to keep everything forever.

337
00:23:34,900 --> 00:23:41,248
At the edge of every event horizon, space itself simmers with particles flickering in and out of existence.

338
00:23:42,167 --> 00:23:47,078
Occasionally one escapes, stealing a whisper of the black hole's mass as it goes.

339
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:49,725
This is Hawking radiation.

340
00:23:49,725 --> 00:23:54,435
A black hole the mass of our sun glows at sixty billionths of a degree.

341
00:23:54,435 --> 00:23:56,359
And it is utterly relentless.

342
00:23:57,300 --> 00:24:01,829
A solar-mass black hole takes ten to the power sixty-seven years to evaporate.

343
00:24:01,829 --> 00:24:05,894
Every year since the Big Bang is a rounding error against that number.

344
00:24:06,833 --> 00:24:09,748
But this timelapse does not stop.

345
00:24:09,748 --> 00:24:10,542
So watch.

346
00:24:11,467 --> 00:24:12,987
The black hole shrinks.

347
00:24:12,987 --> 00:24:16,953
And shrinking makes it hotter, which makes it shrink faster.

348
00:24:17,900 --> 00:24:19,367
Slowly at first.

349
00:24:19,367 --> 00:24:22,027
Over eons, the glow sharpens.

350
00:24:22,967 --> 00:24:27,565
The stellar black holes go first, each ending in a burst of gamma rays.

351
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:34,299
One by one, across an eternity, the archives of the universe burn their contents and close.

352
00:24:35,233 --> 00:24:38,715
The supermassive monsters hold out the longest.

353
00:24:38,715 --> 00:24:43,827
A giant like TON 618 lasts nearly ten to the power one hundred years.

354
00:24:44,767 --> 00:24:47,697
A one followed by one hundred zeros.

355
00:24:47,697 --> 00:24:48,429
A googol.

356
00:24:48,429 --> 00:24:52,987
There are not a googol atoms in the observable universe.

357
00:24:52,987 --> 00:24:55,673
There are not a googol anythings.

358
00:24:55,673 --> 00:24:56,731
Except years.

359
00:24:57,667 --> 00:25:03,231
And now, somewhere in the darkness, the last black hole in the universe is dying.

360
00:25:04,167 --> 00:25:06,568
It has outlived everything.

361
00:25:06,568 --> 00:25:09,324
Stars, galaxies, matter itself.

362
00:25:09,324 --> 00:25:12,526
It is the final object in existence.

363
00:25:13,467 --> 00:25:14,959
Its horizon tightens.

364
00:25:14,959 --> 00:25:19,580
Its glow climbs from billionths of a degree toward a final fever.

365
00:25:20,500 --> 00:25:25,024
If you could stand witness, you would see the only light in the universe.

366
00:25:25,024 --> 00:25:31,654
A single point, brightening in an infinite black, like the last coal of a fire that once filled everything.

367
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:37,276
In its final second, it releases the energy of a million hydrogen bombs.

368
00:25:38,200 --> 00:25:39,114
A flash.

369
00:25:40,033 --> 00:25:45,440
The last event horizon evaporates, and the last thing that could ever be called a thing, is gone.

370
00:25:46,367 --> 00:25:50,651
The universe's final firework, with no one and nothing left to see it.

371
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:52,593
Silence.

372
00:25:53,533 --> 00:25:56,302
Beyond ten to the power one hundred years.

373
00:25:57,233 --> 00:25:58,851
This is the Dark Era.

374
00:25:58,851 --> 00:26:04,626
It is the universe's final state, and it lasts, as far as we know, forever.

375
00:26:05,567 --> 00:26:13,378
Nothing remains but stray photons, neutrinos, electrons, and positrons, drifting through expanding emptiness.

376
00:26:14,300 --> 00:26:20,099
The average distance between particles is now larger than the entire observable universe of our time.

377
00:26:21,033 --> 00:26:26,408
Occasionally, an electron and a positron find each other and fall into orbit.

378
00:26:26,408 --> 00:26:29,549
An atom of positronium, larger than a galaxy.

379
00:26:30,467 --> 00:26:34,530
A single orbit takes longer than the entire age of stars.

380
00:26:34,530 --> 00:26:37,311
It is the last slow dance in existence.

381
00:26:38,233 --> 00:26:40,681
Nothing has a temperature anymore.

382
00:26:40,681 --> 00:26:43,273
Nothing has a location worth naming.

383
00:26:43,273 --> 00:26:44,424
Nothing happens.

384
00:26:45,367 --> 00:26:47,131
This is heat death.

385
00:26:47,131 --> 00:26:49,266
Not fire, not collapse.

386
00:26:49,266 --> 00:26:53,073
Just perfect, uniform, eternal stillness.

387
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,146
Time still passes, technically.

388
00:26:56,146 --> 00:27:00,922
But time only means anything when things change, and nothing changes.

389
00:27:01,867 --> 00:27:07,418
Past ten to the power twenty-five hundred years, physics simply runs out of predictions.

390
00:27:07,418 --> 00:27:08,554
The map ends here.

391
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:16,971
The story that began with the Big Bang, that built galaxies and oceans and minds, ends in a place with no story at all.

392
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:21,069
At least, that is the ending we believed in.

393
00:27:21,069 --> 00:27:22,654
Until a few years ago.

394
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:26,917
Because the end of the universe was rewritten while you were alive.

395
00:27:27,867 --> 00:27:36,775
In the mid-2020s, an instrument called DESI mapped tens of millions of galaxies to measure dark energy across cosmic history.

396
00:27:37,700 --> 00:27:43,551
And it found hints, growing toward four sigma, that dark energy is not constant.

397
00:27:43,551 --> 00:27:45,014
It may be weakening.

398
00:27:45,933 --> 00:27:50,738
Every ending you just watched assumes dark energy stays fixed forever.

399
00:27:50,738 --> 00:27:52,934
If it evolves, all bets are off.

400
00:27:53,867 --> 00:27:56,669
A weakening push could slow the expansion.

401
00:27:56,669 --> 00:28:02,540
Change it enough, and the universe could even recollapse, ending in fire instead of ice.

402
00:28:03,467 --> 00:28:05,959
Melodysheep could not have known this.

403
00:28:05,959 --> 00:28:12,714
The textbooks are being rewritten right now, in your lifetime, and the final chapter is genuinely open.

404
00:28:13,633 --> 00:28:16,152
The old rivals have weakened, too.

405
00:28:16,152 --> 00:28:22,227
The Big Rip, dark energy tearing space apart, is now disfavored by that same data.

406
00:28:23,167 --> 00:28:32,258
Vacuum decay, a bubble of new physics erasing everything at light speed, stays possible but is expected to wait past ten to the power one hundred years.

407
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:37,484
The apocalypse you would never see coming is, mercifully, in no hurry.

408
00:28:38,433 --> 00:28:41,747
So the quiet ending remains the default.

409
00:28:41,747 --> 00:28:44,232
Expansion, dilution, darkness.

410
00:28:45,167 --> 00:28:46,378
Not with a bang.

411
00:28:46,378 --> 00:28:48,876
With an endless, dimming whisper.

412
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:52,804
But even that whisper may not be the last word.

413
00:28:53,733 --> 00:28:57,050
Because even perfect emptiness is not truly empty.

414
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:01,327
Quantum mechanics forbids absolute stillness.

415
00:29:01,327 --> 00:29:05,393
The vacuum itself always trembles, everywhere, forever.

416
00:29:06,333 --> 00:29:11,819
And given infinite time, even the most absurdly improbable tremble eventually happens.

417
00:29:12,767 --> 00:29:17,913
Some physicists speculate that a large enough quantum fluctuation could seed something extraordinary.

418
00:29:18,833 --> 00:29:20,352
A new inflation event.

419
00:29:20,352 --> 00:29:24,632
A new bubble of spacetime, inflating out of the ashes of ours.

420
00:29:25,567 --> 00:29:28,936
This is speculation stacked on speculation.

421
00:29:28,936 --> 00:29:30,582
Be honest about that.

422
00:29:30,582 --> 00:29:31,601
No one knows.

423
00:29:32,533 --> 00:29:37,601
But if it is possible, then somewhere in that infinite quiet, it is inevitable.

424
00:29:38,533 --> 00:29:41,037
There are other doors out of the dark.

425
00:29:41,037 --> 00:29:47,362
Some cosmologists propose cyclic universes, endlessly renewing, with our eon just one heartbeat.

426
00:29:48,300 --> 00:29:54,569
Roger Penrose has argued that a totally empty universe forgets its own scale, and becomes a new beginning.

427
00:29:55,500 --> 00:30:01,299
In his picture, the infinitely far future and a new Big Bang are literally the same thing.

428
00:30:02,233 --> 00:30:04,417
And mathematics offers one last door.

429
00:30:04,417 --> 00:30:12,029
Wait ten to the power ten to the power ten to the power fifty-six years, and physics suggests arrangements of matter could recur.

430
00:30:12,967 --> 00:30:17,434
That is the largest number with a physical meaning ever written down.

431
00:30:17,434 --> 00:30:18,923
And infinity dwarfs it.

432
00:30:19,867 --> 00:30:21,781
None of this is proven.

433
00:30:21,781 --> 00:30:27,024
All of it is honest physics, published, argued, and unresolved.

434
00:30:27,967 --> 00:30:29,891
A flicker in the dark.

435
00:30:29,891 --> 00:30:32,252
A point of impossible heat.

436
00:30:32,252 --> 00:30:34,001
And then, expansion.

437
00:30:34,933 --> 00:30:41,542
A newborn universe, igniting with its own Big Bang, growing its own galaxies, its own stars.

438
00:30:42,467 --> 00:30:44,015
Perhaps its own oceans.

439
00:30:44,015 --> 00:30:48,188
Perhaps its own minds, looking up and asking how it all began.

440
00:30:49,133 --> 00:30:51,401
Perhaps this has already happened.

441
00:30:51,401 --> 00:30:54,201
Perhaps our own Big Bang was exactly this.

442
00:30:55,133 --> 00:31:00,775
The last moment of one universe, and the first moment of the next, might be the same moment.

443
00:31:01,700 --> 00:31:05,253
The end of time might just be time, clearing its throat.

444
00:31:06,200 --> 00:31:07,036
Now come back.

445
00:31:07,967 --> 00:31:13,688
Back past the dark era, the dying black holes, the dissolving atoms, the last star.

446
00:31:14,633 --> 00:31:19,152
Back past the frozen Earth, the swollen sun, the waltzing galaxies.

447
00:31:20,100 --> 00:31:26,291
Back to a small blue planet, in a bright young galaxy, 13.8 billion years after the beginning.

448
00:31:27,233 --> 00:31:28,330
Back to now.

449
00:31:28,330 --> 00:31:29,427
Back to you.

450
00:31:30,367 --> 00:31:33,528
Here is the thing this entire journey was building to.

451
00:31:34,467 --> 00:31:37,275
Run the numbers on everything you just watched.

452
00:31:37,275 --> 00:31:40,501
Compare the age of stars to the eternity that follows.

453
00:31:41,433 --> 00:31:47,389
Life like ours is possible for only the thinnest sliver at the very start of the universe's story.

454
00:31:48,333 --> 00:31:50,797
Physicist Brian Cox put it this way.

455
00:31:50,797 --> 00:31:55,246
The arrow of time creates a brief window in which life can exist.

456
00:31:55,246 --> 00:31:56,614
This is that window.

457
00:31:57,533 --> 00:32:00,809
You are not living at some random point in eternity.

458
00:32:00,809 --> 00:32:03,959
You are living in the flash at the very beginning.

459
00:32:04,900 --> 00:32:06,839
The universe is not ancient.

460
00:32:06,839 --> 00:32:10,856
Compared to what is coming, it has barely opened its eyes.

461
00:32:11,800 --> 00:32:14,133
You live in the era of light.

462
00:32:14,133 --> 00:32:18,879
The brief, dazzling age when stars burn and skies are full.

463
00:32:19,800 --> 00:32:25,678
The age when the universe can still be seen, still be read, still be understood.

464
00:32:26,600 --> 00:32:32,007
Beings in the far future, if any exist, would trade anything to see what you see tonight.

465
00:32:32,933 --> 00:32:35,428
A sky full of stars is not normal.

466
00:32:35,428 --> 00:32:39,464
It is a temporary miracle, and you were born inside it.

467
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:46,278
Every sunrise you have ever ignored is a spectacle the last nine-tenths of eternity will never get.

468
00:32:47,200 --> 00:32:49,859
And here is the strangest comfort of all.

469
00:32:49,859 --> 00:32:52,842
Nothing that happens in this video erases you.

470
00:32:53,767 --> 00:32:56,237
The future does not delete the past.

471
00:32:56,237 --> 00:32:57,816
It only comes after it.

472
00:32:58,733 --> 00:33:03,487
Ten to the power one hundred years from now, it will still be true that you existed.

473
00:33:04,433 --> 00:33:09,919
That you lived on a blue world, under a young sun, in the era when the universe was awake.

474
00:33:10,867 --> 00:33:13,299
The heat death cannot touch that.

475
00:33:13,299 --> 00:33:14,184
Nothing can.

476
00:33:15,133 --> 00:33:22,212
The universe spent 13.8 billion years arranging atoms into something that could watch its own ending and feel awe.

477
00:33:23,133 --> 00:33:24,294
You are not small.

478
00:33:24,294 --> 00:33:27,260
You are the part of the universe that noticed.

479
